WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.000
Hey everyone, welcome to AI Agent 101.

00:05.000 --> 00:11.000
Today we're going to take a fun practical look at what makes large language models more than just chatbots,

00:11.000 --> 00:16.000
and how to turn them into powerful open source agents that can actually do things.

00:16.000 --> 00:24.000
To warm up, let's look at a real open source example built by a project called WebUI by Browser Use.

00:24.000 --> 00:27.000
Here's a scenario. Say I want to cook chili for dinner.

00:27.000 --> 00:31.000
So I'm going to prompt the agent, I want to cook chili for dinner tonight.

00:31.000 --> 00:35.000
Can you find the ingredients and put them in my shopping cart?

00:35.000 --> 00:38.000
This open source agent plans the workflow for me.

00:38.000 --> 00:43.000
It finds the recipe, extracts the ingredients, and adds them to the cart automatically.

00:43.000 --> 00:51.000
Watch how it alternates between thinking and doing, reasoning about the recipe, then browsing online, then taking action to fill the basket.

00:51.000 --> 00:56.000
That's the React agent pattern in motion, combining reasoning with real-world action.

00:56.000 --> 01:03.000
Instead of just answering questions, the model becomes a practical assistant that helps you actually accomplish things.

01:03.000 --> 01:05.000
Here's an important distinction.

01:05.000 --> 01:09.000
An LLM on its own, it's like a really smart book.

01:09.000 --> 01:13.000
It can answer questions, but it can't actually do anything.

01:13.000 --> 01:17.000
An agent takes that same LLM and treats it as the brain.

01:17.000 --> 01:21.000
The brain can reason, plan, and make decisions.

01:21.000 --> 01:23.000
But to act, it needs tools.

01:23.000 --> 01:29.000
What an agent is capable of depends entirely on what tools and capabilities we give it.

01:29.000 --> 01:36.000
If the agent only has a calculator tool, it can only act and perform for math-related actions.

01:36.000 --> 01:39.000
If you add a browser tool, now it can search online.

01:39.000 --> 01:43.000
And if you add shopping APIs, suddenly it can buy groceries for you.

01:43.000 --> 01:45.000
Let's look at an example.

01:45.000 --> 01:48.000
Agent A has only a calendar tool.

01:48.000 --> 01:51.000
It can help you schedule meetings, but it can't check the weather.

01:51.000 --> 01:56.000
Now, Agent B has a calendar tool, the weather tool, and a browser tool.

01:56.000 --> 02:04.000
Now, not only you can schedule your meetings, but also check the weather and even find the best cafes nearby.

02:04.000 --> 02:06.000
Same brain, different tools.

02:06.000 --> 02:09.000
The difference is not in the LLM itself.

02:09.000 --> 02:11.000
It's the toolbox we provide it to.

02:11.000 --> 02:13.000
That's the magic of agents.

02:13.000 --> 02:17.000
LLMs provide the reasoning, but the tools define the actions.

02:17.000 --> 02:21.000
The more useful the tools, the more capable the agents become.

02:21.000 --> 02:27.000
The React paradigm is what turns an LLM from a passive Q&A system into an active agent.

02:27.000 --> 02:29.000
And here's how it works.

02:29.000 --> 02:32.000
For the reasoning part, the LLM thinks about the problem.

02:32.000 --> 02:39.000
For example, for the Chile example, it knows that it needs to find the ingredients for Chile.

02:39.000 --> 02:41.000
The action part is based on that reasoning.

02:41.000 --> 02:48.000
It calls a tool, searching online, grabbing a list of ingredients, or maybe even calling a shopping API.

02:48.000 --> 02:53.000
And for the loop, the agent alternates between reasoning and acting.

02:53.000 --> 02:57.000
It checks the progress and continues until the goal is achieved.

02:57.000 --> 03:03.000
This React, which is Reason plus Act cycle, is simple, but powerful.

03:03.000 --> 03:06.000
It's the foundation of almost every modern agent framework.

03:06.000 --> 03:11.000
It lets us move from static answers to real-world actions.

03:11.000 --> 03:17.000
To build agents around the React loop, we rely on frameworks that handle orchestration.

03:17.000 --> 03:22.000
Pydantic AI is a choice that we made for this course, but there is more than Pydantic AI.

03:22.000 --> 03:27.000
Pydantic AI is a simple and easy for developers to build reliable agents.

03:27.000 --> 03:32.000
There is more frameworks such as LandGraph, which is specialized in chaining and orchestration.

03:32.000 --> 03:37.000
There is OpenAI SDK, Crew AI, and more.

03:37.000 --> 03:41.000
Each framework fits a different level of complexity. The choice depends on your need.

03:41.000 --> 03:47.000
With AMD GPU, as long as you power the LLM, you can choose any of these open source frameworks.

03:47.000 --> 03:53.000
Another key piece of this puzzle is MCP, the model context protocol.

03:53.000 --> 03:59.000
Think of MCP as the standardized wiring between your agent and the outside world.

03:59.000 --> 04:07.000
Instead of hard coding every integration, MCP provides a common language so LLMs can discover and use tools in a consistent way.

04:07.000 --> 04:18.000
That means you can connect your agent to external APIs like calendars, weather services, or databases, without writing a custom adapter for each.

04:18.000 --> 04:28.000
You can reuse pre-built MCP servers. For example, if someone already built a weather MCP server, you can just plug it in and your agent instantly has weather capabilities.

04:28.000 --> 04:35.000
In short, MCP lowers the friction. It makes it much easier to expand what your agents can do.

04:35.000 --> 04:38.000
Finally, the model, the brain, or LLM.

04:38.000 --> 04:43.000
If you're running agents at scale, you don't want to be locked into expensive cloud endpoints.

04:43.000 --> 04:52.000
With open source inference engines like VLLM and SGLANG, you can serve the model of your choice with an OpenAI compatible API.

04:52.000 --> 04:59.000
That gives you flexibility to run open source models like Quint3, GPT-OSS, or DeepSeq.

04:59.000 --> 05:05.000
You can deploy them on AMD GPUs and then connect them to your AI agentic framework of your choice.

05:05.000 --> 05:14.000
Let's get hands on. We'll walk through this notebook, starting with spinning a VLLM server on an AMD GPU,

05:14.000 --> 05:24.000
then connecting to our Pedantic AI agent, and finally wiring an NCP to give it real-world capabilities.

05:24.000 --> 05:29.000
VLLM is an open source inference engine that's fully OpenAI compatible.

05:29.000 --> 05:38.000
It allows you to run the model on GPUs such as AMD and SGLANG MI300X, and serve models like Quint3.

05:38.000 --> 05:44.000
This creates a local API, so you can connect it to your agentic framework of choice.

05:44.000 --> 05:52.000
Once you've started your VLLM server, and you have the endpoint ready, now we can define our agent.

05:52.000 --> 05:56.000
Pydantic AI wraps our model and connects it to an agent instance.

05:56.000 --> 06:00.000
At this point, the agent can reason, but can't take any actions.

06:00.000 --> 06:03.000
It's a pure LLM brain with no tools.

06:03.000 --> 06:06.000
So, let's say if you ask, what's the date today?

06:06.000 --> 06:09.000
It'll fail because it has no access to real-time data.

06:09.000 --> 06:12.000
Let's fix that by giving it a tool.

06:12.000 --> 06:15.000
The ability to check the current date.

06:15.000 --> 06:21.000
We define a simple function, decorate it with tool, and add it to our agent.

06:21.000 --> 06:27.000
Now, when the model encounters a time-related question, it can decide to call the function.

06:27.000 --> 06:30.000
This is the essence of tool calling.

06:30.000 --> 06:35.000
The model decides when and why to use the tool, based on its reasoning.

06:35.000 --> 06:37.000
Let's test it out.

06:37.000 --> 06:40.000
When we ask, what's the date today?

06:40.000 --> 06:45.000
The agent uses the tool, gets the current time, and continues the conversation.

06:45.000 --> 06:49.000
So now, we've officially built our first reasoning acting loop.

06:49.000 --> 06:53.000
Now it's time to scale this idea with MCP, the model context protocol.

06:53.000 --> 06:59.000
As we said before, MCP standardizes how agents discover and use tools.

06:59.000 --> 07:03.000
You can think of it like a package manager for agent tool.

07:03.000 --> 07:13.000
Instead of manually writing Python functions, you can connect a ready-made MCP server that exposes multiple tools through one unified interface.

07:13.000 --> 07:16.000
Here's what happens under the hood.

07:16.000 --> 07:21.000
The agent framework, like Pydantic AI, acts as MCP client.

07:21.000 --> 07:26.000
It connects to one or more MCP servers, each of which provides a tool set.

07:26.000 --> 07:35.000
For example, one server might offer get current time, another Airbnb search, another weather forecast.

07:35.000 --> 07:44.000
These servers expose the standard JSON schemas describing what each tool does, and what parameters it needs, and what output to expect.

07:44.000 --> 07:55.000
The agent dynamically queries these schemas, decides which tool to use, sends a request, and processes the response, all in the same reasoning loop.

07:55.000 --> 07:58.000
This makes MCP incredibly powerful.

07:58.000 --> 08:06.000
Instead of hard coding tool logic, you can plug in a pack of tools, and your agent instantly gains new abilities.

08:06.000 --> 08:12.000
In our notebook, we'll replace our local get current date tool with an MCP server.

08:12.000 --> 08:20.000
Then we'll plug in another MCP server, the Airbnb MCP, to let the agent browse listings.

08:20.000 --> 08:26.000
Once you go through the notebook and add Airbnb and time MCPs, you can test out the agent.

08:26.000 --> 08:30.000
Now our agent can browse real Airbnb listings.

08:30.000 --> 08:36.000
When we ask, find a place to stay in Vancouver next Sunday for three nights for two adults.

08:36.000 --> 08:39.000
The agent goes through the loop of reasoning.

08:39.000 --> 08:44.000
It first gets the current date from the time server that we define.

08:44.000 --> 08:47.000
Then it calculates when next Sunday is going to be.

08:47.000 --> 08:51.000
Then it uses the Airbnb search to find listings.

08:51.000 --> 08:54.000
It gets the details from the Airbnb listing details.

08:54.000 --> 08:58.000
And all dynamically happening within the loop.

08:58.000 --> 09:01.000
So you can get the final answers from your agent.

09:01.000 --> 09:05.000
Now that you've built the foundation, the challenge is to expand the agent.

09:05.000 --> 09:13.000
For example, you can integrate the weather MCP server so your agent can check the weather forecast to suggest traveling dates.

09:13.000 --> 09:15.000
The pattern stays the same.

09:15.000 --> 09:19.000
Spin up a new MCP server, just like what we did before, and add it to the toolset.

09:19.000 --> 09:22.000
And let the agent decide when to call it.

09:22.000 --> 09:28.000
Because MCP is a shared protocol, you can discover thousands of compatible servers online.

09:28.000 --> 09:34.000
This modular design means your agent can grow over time, gaining new skills without rewriting code.

09:34.000 --> 09:39.000
So to recap, LLMs become agents when they can reason and act.

09:39.000 --> 09:43.000
Frameworks like Pedantic AI manage this reasoning action loop.

09:43.000 --> 09:48.000
MCP provides a universal interface to connect any tool or API.

09:48.000 --> 09:55.000
And AMD GPUs give you the open, high-performance backbone to run it all efficiently.

09:55.000 --> 10:02.000
With these tools, you can go from static chatbots to dynamic AI systems that truly interact with the real world.

10:02.000 --> 10:08.000
You can plug in hundreds of tools, host your own inference, and control the entire agentic stack.

10:08.000 --> 10:10.000
All open, all yours.

10:10.000 --> 10:13.000
Thank you for watching, and now let's start building.

